1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,768
Aarav puts new batteries
into his bike lamp.

2
00:00:03,118 --> 00:00:04,645
He clicks the switch.

3
00:00:04,645 --> 00:00:05,804
Nothing happens.

4
00:00:06,154 --> 00:00:08,154
He flips the batteries around.

5
00:00:08,154 --> 00:00:09,255
Now it shines!

6
00:00:09,755 --> 00:00:13,242
Inside the lamp is a
diode, a p-n junction.

7
00:00:13,592 --> 00:00:15,954
It lets current through one way only.

8
00:00:15,954 --> 00:00:17,398
Let's see exactly how.

9
00:00:18,048 --> 00:00:20,744
Think of a heavy swing
door with a strong spring.

10
00:00:21,094 --> 00:00:23,036
Push it gently from the front.

11
00:00:23,036 --> 00:00:24,270
It does not move.

12
00:00:24,620 --> 00:00:28,562
Push harder, past a certain
point, and it swings wide open.

13
00:00:29,162 --> 00:00:31,130
Push from the back, and it stays shut.

14
00:00:31,730 --> 00:00:33,986
Unless you push so hard that you break it.

15
00:00:34,636 --> 00:00:36,100
Now the real diode.

16
00:00:36,450 --> 00:00:40,476
Connect p to the plus side of
a battery, n to the minus side.

17
00:00:41,076 --> 00:00:42,909
This is called forward bias.

18
00:00:43,509 --> 00:00:47,164
The battery pushes against the
barrier, so the wall gets lower.

19
00:00:47,764 --> 00:00:49,523
The depletion layer gets thinner.

20
00:00:50,123 --> 00:00:53,378
Once the push beats the
barrier, carriers flood across.

21
00:00:54,028 --> 00:00:55,684
Now flip the battery.

22
00:00:55,684 --> 00:00:57,631
p to minus, n to plus.

23
00:00:57,981 --> 00:00:59,697
This is reverse bias.

24
00:01:00,297 --> 00:01:02,910
Now the battery pulls carriers
away from the junction.

25
00:01:03,510 --> 00:01:06,632
The depletion layer gets wider,
and the wall gets higher.

26
00:01:07,232 --> 00:01:10,686
Only a tiny leak gets through,
carried by the few minority carriers.

27
00:01:11,336 --> 00:01:13,346
Let's measure it on the breadboard.

28
00:01:13,696 --> 00:01:18,313
A variable supply, a resistor to
protect the diode, and the diode.

29
00:01:18,663 --> 00:01:20,645
A voltmeter across the diode.

30
00:01:20,645 --> 00:01:22,379
A meter for the current.

31
00:01:22,729 --> 00:01:25,703
Turn the knob up slowly, in forward bias.

32
00:01:26,053 --> 00:01:28,031
Point two, point four volts.

33
00:01:28,031 --> 00:01:29,314
Almost no current.

34
00:01:29,914 --> 00:01:32,633
Near point seven volts,
the current shoots up.

35
00:01:33,233 --> 00:01:34,985
This bend is the knee.

36
00:01:34,985 --> 00:01:38,744
The voltage there is the
threshold, or cut-in voltage.

37
00:01:39,344 --> 00:01:42,107
For silicon it is about point seven volts.

38
00:01:42,107 --> 00:01:44,254
For germanium, about point two.

39
00:01:44,904 --> 00:01:47,486
Now reverse the supply and turn it up.

40
00:01:47,836 --> 00:01:51,020
The current stays tiny, just
microamperes, and almost flat.

41
00:01:51,620 --> 00:01:55,246
Then, at the breakdown
voltage, it suddenly shoots up.

42
00:01:55,846 --> 00:01:58,151
Ordinary diodes are not
meant to work there.

43
00:01:58,151 --> 00:01:59,850
Too much heat can destroy them.

44
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:02,399
Look at the scales on the graph.

45
00:02:02,749 --> 00:02:04,577
Forward current is in milliamperes.

46
00:02:04,577 --> 00:02:06,405
Reverse current is in microamperes.

47
00:02:06,405 --> 00:02:07,788
A thousand times smaller.

48
00:02:08,438 --> 00:02:11,203
A diode is a one-way door with a doorstep.

49
00:02:12,003 --> 00:02:14,076
The curve is not a straight line.

50
00:02:14,076 --> 00:02:16,248
So a diode does not obey Ohm's law.

51
00:02:16,598 --> 00:02:18,773
So we use dynamic resistance.

52
00:02:19,123 --> 00:02:23,242
It is a small change in voltage
divided by the change in current.

53
00:02:23,842 --> 00:02:27,094
r d equals delta V divided by delta I.

54
00:02:27,744 --> 00:02:28,725
Worked example.

55
00:02:28,725 --> 00:02:30,433
Two readings above the knee.

56
00:02:30,783 --> 00:02:34,146
At point seven two volts, the
current is twelve milliamperes.

57
00:02:34,746 --> 00:02:37,869
At point seven six volts,
it is twenty milliamperes.

58
00:02:38,469 --> 00:02:40,711
Delta V is point zero four volts.

59
00:02:40,711 --> 00:02:42,692
Delta I is eight milliamperes.

60
00:02:43,392 --> 00:02:47,526
r d equals point zero four
divided by point zero zero eight.

61
00:02:48,226 --> 00:02:49,910
That is just five ohms.

62
00:02:49,910 --> 00:02:52,148
Forward, the door is wide open.

63
00:02:52,848 --> 00:02:55,734
In reverse, ten volts
drives only one microampere.

64
00:02:55,734 --> 00:02:57,294
That is ten million ohms!

65
00:02:57,944 --> 00:02:59,406
Now, the exam trap.

66
00:02:59,756 --> 00:03:02,931
Many students treat a diode
like a fixed resistor.

67
00:03:02,931 --> 00:03:03,831
It is not.

68
00:03:04,179 --> 00:03:06,867
Its resistance changes along the curve.

69
00:03:06,867 --> 00:03:09,251
Always use delta V over delta I.

70
00:03:09,901 --> 00:03:11,041
Where is this asked?

71
00:03:11,041 --> 00:03:14,353
PUC, CBSE and ISC boards ask you
to draw and explain this curve.

72
00:03:14,703 --> 00:03:19,137
JEE Main asks for dynamic resistance
from the graph, as in June twenty

73
00:03:19,137 --> 00:03:20,058
twenty two.

74
00:03:20,708 --> 00:03:21,608
Try this.

75
00:03:21,560 --> 00:03:26,436
A five volt battery, a silicon diode
in forward bias, and a four hundred

76
00:03:26,436 --> 00:03:28,838
thirty ohm resistor are in series.

77
00:03:28,838 --> 00:03:31,239
The diode drops point seven volts.

78
00:03:31,239 --> 00:03:32,570
Find the current.

79
00:03:37,970 --> 00:03:38,870
A.

80
00:03:38,147 --> 00:03:39,047
Ten milliamperes.

81
00:03:39,024 --> 00:03:41,251
The diode takes point seven volts.

82
00:03:41,601 --> 00:03:45,648
So the resistor gets five minus point
seven, which is four point three

83
00:03:45,648 --> 00:03:46,548
volts.

84
00:03:46,555 --> 00:03:49,943
Current is four point three
divided by four hundred thirty.

85
00:03:49,943 --> 00:03:51,494
That is ten milliamperes.

86
00:03:52,144 --> 00:03:53,691
Screenshot this card.

87
00:03:56,891 --> 00:03:58,836
Next, the half-wave rectifier.

88
00:03:58,836 --> 00:04:01,448
How does a one-way door turn AC into DC?
